    "The Merchant shall be liable for all fines, penalties, costs, expenses and losses which the Carrier, Vessel or cargo may incur through nonobservance of Customs House and/or import or export regulations.",
    "The Carrier is entitled in case of incorrect declaration of contents, weights, measurements or value of the cargo to claim double the amount of freight which would have been due if such declaration had been correctly given. For the purpose of ascertaining the actual facts, the Carrier shall have the right to obtain from the Merchant the original invoice and to have the cargo inspected and its contents, weight, measurement or value verified.",
    "13. Lien.",
    "(a) The Carrier shall have a lien on the cargo and on all sub-freights payable in respect of the cargo for freight, deadfreight, demurrage, claims for damages and for all other amounts due under this Bill of Lading and all costs of recovering same, including legal costs.",
    "(b) Without prejudice to subclause (a) above, if upon the Vessel’s arrival at or off a port or place of discharge any freight then due and payable has not been received by the Carrier, the Carrier shall be entitled to hold the Vessel at a safe place outside or inside territorial waters until such payment has been received in full, and any resulting loss of time shall be compensated by the Carrier. The Merchant shall reimburse the Carrier for the cost of any deviation insurance, and shall indemnify the Carrier against any claim raised by any third party (including the costs of defending the same), and promptly provide appropriate security or substitute security to obtain the Vessel’s unimpeded departure in the event of its actual or threatened arrest or detention.",
    "14. General Average and Salvage.",
    "General Average shall be adjusted, stated and settled in Paris according to the York-Antwerp Rules 2016, in respect of all cargo, whether carried on or under deck. In the event of accident, danger, damage or disaster before or after commencement of the carriage resulting from any cause whatsoever, whether due to negligence or not, for which or for the consequence of which the Carrier is not responsible by statute, contract or otherwise, the Merchant shall contribute with the Carrier in General Average to the payment of any sacrifice, losses or expenses of a General Average nature that may be made or incurred, and shall pay salvage and special charges incurred in respect of the cargo. If a salving vessel is owned or operated by the Carrier, salvage shall be paid for as fully as if the salving vessel or vessels belonged to strangers.",
    "15. Both-to-Blame Collision Clause.",
    "If the Vessel comes into collision with another vessel as a result of the negligence of the other vessel and any act, negligence or default of the Master, Mariner, Pilot or the servants of the Carrier in the navigation or in the management of the Vessel, the Merchant will indemnify the Carrier against all loss or liability to the other or non-carrying vessel or her Owner in so far as such loss or liability represents loss of or damage to or any claim whatsoever of the owner of the cargo paid or payable by the other or non-carrying vessel or her Owner to the owner of the cargo and set-off, recouped or recovered by the other or non-carrying vessel or her Owner as part of its claim against the carrying vessel or Carrier. The foregoing provisions shall also apply where the Owner, operator or those in charge of any vessel or vessels or objects other than, or in addition to, the colliding vessels or objects are at fault in respect of a collision or contact.",
    "16. Laycan, ETA and Noon Position Notices, and Cancellation",
    "(a)The Merchant shall give Carrier a proposed Laycan of minimum fourteen (14) days (the “Proposed Laycan Period”) at the Loading Port minimum thirty (30) days prior to the first day of the proposed Laycan Period. The Merchant shall confirm to the Carrier the Proposed Laycan Period within maximum five (5) days of receipt of the Carrier’s Proposed Laycan Period or propose an Adjusted Laycan Period. If the Carrier fails to respond within five (5) days, the carrier’s Proposed Laycan Period shall be the Agreed Laycan. If the Carrier propose an Adjusted Laycan Period, provided that such Adjusted Laycan Period falls within a fourteen (14) day period both ends of the Merchant’s Proposed Laycan Period, the Adjusted Laycan Period shall become the Agreed Laycan.",
    "(b)The Carrier shall give the Merchant Estimated Time of Arrival notices (“ETA Notices”) at the Port of Loading and, after sailing from the Port of Loading, at the Port of Discharge, and Noon Position Notices (Latitude and Longitude position of the Vessel at 1200 UT) every day from the seventh (7th) day prior to the first day of the Agreed Laycan until completion of the carriage.",
    "(c)Should the Vessel not be ready to load (whether in berth or not) by the last day of the Agreed Laycan plus a free margin of 48 hours (the Cancelling Date”), the Merchant shall have the option of cancelling the carriage under this Bill of lading at no cost which shall be the Merchant’s sole and exclusive remedy for all and any losses flowing from the non-performed voyage.",
    "(d)Should the Cargo not be ready to be loaded on the Vessel within 72 hours of the Vessel’s tendering its Notice of Readiness, the Vessel shall have the option of sailing without the Cargo in which case the voyage shall be deemed performed and Freight fully payable as dead freight together with any accrued demurrage and any other sums due under the Bill of Lading for the relevant voyage.",
    "(e)Should the Carrier anticipate that despite the exercise of due diligence, the Vessel will not be ready to load by the Cancelling Date, they shall notify the Merchant thereof without delay stating the expected date of the Vessel’s readiness to load and asking whether the Merchant will exercise the option of cancelling the carriage, or agree to a new cancelling date. Such option must be declared by the Merchant within 48 hours of receipt of Carrier’s notice. If the Merchant do not exercise their option of cancelling, then this Bill of lading shall be deemed to be amended such that the fourteenth (14th) day after the new readiness date stated in Carrier’s notification to the Merchant shall be the new Cancelling Date.",
    "(f)Should the Merchant anticipate that despite the exercise of due diligence, the Cargo will not be ready to load by the Vessel’s ETA Notice, they shall notify the Carrier thereof without delay stating the expected date of the Cargo’s readiness to load and asking whether the Carrier will exercise the option of sailing without the cargo on board or will be prepared to wait until the expected Cargo’s readiness date. The Carrier shall always consider in good faith whether it is possible to accommodate the Merchant’s expected Cargo’s readiness date to the extent possible always without compromising the Vessel’s schedule and next carriages (which shall have priority) and always without prejudice to paragraph (e) above.",
    "17. Government directions, War, Epidemics, Ice, Strikes, etc.",
